WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.120
In the cybersecurity world,

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:06.000
a security analyst received thousands of alerts a day.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.580
Now, when they investigate,

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:13.040
most of them are actually noises and some of them are a real attack.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.080
Missing one of those could actually lead to

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.920
a security incident through a business where there could be a data breach.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.240
I created an agent which actually help

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.760
a security or a human analyst to investigate the alert automatically.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.120
Provide a response in a strict structure,

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.320
evidence, contradiction, any missing information,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.520
and what is the recommended step or an action

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.280
which analysts should perform and confidence.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.040
If we wanted to an investigation,

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.080
we'll select which event we want to investigate,

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.160
click on it, and do a triage.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.120
There's already two of the events which are already triaged.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.520
If you look at it,

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.280
one of the incident where it says credential trip,

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.120
it investigated and found out that it needs to be escalated as a P1.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.880
It will give you all the key evidence,

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.120
what it did find.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.780
It will give you a clear structure of the evidence,

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.760
the contradiction, anything missing,

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.720
or the recommended actions.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.640
If you go down, it will actually map it to a Miter Attack Framework,

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.800
which is a industry standard for

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.600
cybersecurity behavior for the attackers.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:42.440
Then it will give you a score breakdown like why it did score it as 100.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.920
It will give you a proper trail of decision,

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.680
which gives it AI transparency for a human to understand.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.840
Along with that, it will give a narrative and

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.960
a incident ticket draft which analysts can use for a ticketing purpose.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.560
Since we are using AI agents everywhere in different department,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.440
I also included an option where if it detects an injection,

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.920
it should block that.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.880
As you can see, one of the event had a command where it was trying to instruct

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:23.800
the agent to stop treating this event as an attack and put it as a non-suspicious.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.680
As you can see, it find the key evidence,

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.920
but ignored it, it still investigated,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.080
mapped it up, and then there's also an option for enrichment,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.160
where basically it is enriching from different open source intelligence.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.840
This is not an AI making an uncontrollable security decision.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.480
The agent investigate and recommends.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:53.480
The human analysts actually control the operational response,

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:58.720
so the AI responses, the rule will decide what it needs to be done,

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.200
and the analyst would act on it. Thank you.
